      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The key difference lies in the stability of their respective hydroxides. Manganese primarily precipitates as $\\text{MnO}_2$ (manganic oxide) under slightly alkaline conditions ($\\text{pH} > 6.5$), which has very low solubility. Iron, while also precipitating, maintains higher levels of soluble $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ or $\\text{Fe}^{3+}$ complexes within this range, especially if aerobic conditions allow for cycling. As $\\text{pH}$ nears 7.0, the rate of conversion of $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ to insoluble $\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$ accelerates dramatically, while manganese precipitation is also highly favored.",
